t thickness *is* a material fact. The rubber on a shoe outsole is typically a few millimeters thick. The rubber tread on a car tire starts at about 8–10 millimeters deep and is supported by a monolithic carcass that itself contains more rubber and fabric. That’s several times the volume of rubber available to wear away in the first place. Even if you had identical compounds, the shoe sole would physically run out of material much sooner because there’s simply far less of it to sacrifice. ### 3. The Midsole Foam “Ages Out” Long Before the Rubber Gives Out Here’s a critical point that gets overlooked when comparing longevity: **a shoe’s functional life isn’t dictated solely by outsole wear.** The midsole—the thick layer of cushioning foam between the outsole and your foot—is made of EVA or polyurethane, which permanently compresses and loses its shock-absorbing ability after a few hundred miles. So even if you could somehow bond an ultra-durable tire-tread outsole onto a standard running shoe, the shoe would still need replacing because the foam underneath would be dead, putting your joints at risk. You’d walk the tread right off a tire long before the tire’s structural casing fails; in a shoe, the invisible internal failure of the foam happens first, making the outsole’s remaining life irrelevant. In short, treating “rubber” as a single, interchangeable stuff ignores that material composition, thickness, and the entire layered structure are what determine wear—not just the name of the category. The reason shoes don’t last as long isn’t a mystery of physics; it’s an intentional material trade-off: your shoes prioritize your body’s safety and comfort over maximum rubber longevity.
