s dramatically. A tire wears out from the relentless friction of thousands of miles at high speed; a boot might see far less actual sliding distance per year. So the *chronological* lifespan can match up because the usage intensity is completely different. ### 3. The Tire Equivalent to Your Dad’s Boots If you want a direct apples-to-apples comparison, imagine this: - Take a tire tread compound, shape it into a boot sole 15mm thick, with no soft foam underneath. - Now walk 5 miles a day on rough concrete. That "boot" would indeed last for years, but it would be punishingly uncomfortable and transmit every shock to your knees. Your dad’s boots likely felt broken-in and supportive because of the leather footbed, but they still lacked the impact absorption of modern athletic shoes. That’s the trade-off. ### 4. Tires Age Out, Too Even a tire that sits unused for 8 years will often need replacement due to dry rot, cracking, and hardening—exactly the same type of chemical aging that makes old boot soles crack. If you find a pair of pristine 8-year-old tires in a garage, they’d be unsafe. So the comparable lifespan isn’t just about wear; it’s also about an aging mechanism that affects both rubber types eventually. So your observation doesn’t contradict the material science—it highlights it. When a shoe uses thick, tire-like rubber, strips away the disposable foam, and is worn at a human pace rather than driven at highway speeds, *of course* it can last as long as a tire. But that kind of shoe is an entirely different beast from the ones that wear out in 6 months. It’s not that "rubber is rubber"; it’s that the boots your dad had were designed with the same brute-force durability philosophy as tires, while most shoes aren’t.
