bility. Companies give us what we overwhelmingly demand: shoes that feel good and protect our bodies. If they sold tire-soled, zero-cushion boots to the mass market, they’d fail because nobody would wear them. Your original point was “they should be able to last as long as tires.” Technologically, one *component* (the outsole) could. But a functional, comfortable, safe shoe cannot, because the very things that make it comfortable and safe are inherently shorter-lived. That’s not a corporate swindle—it’s a trade-off written into physics and biology.
