22:32:23 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I see why that story resonates—grandparents’ tales of things “built to last” often have a kernel of truth, and they feel like a lost virtue. But your grandpa’s memory isn’t quite evidence that foam is a gimmick that *robbed* shoes of tire-like durability. It actually shows a different trade-off, not a conspiracy. Let’s break down what those old “solid” shoes really were, and why today’s shoes don’t last as long in the same way. ### 1. Old shoes *did* last longer structurally, but at a cost Your grandpa is right: leather-soled dress shoes or sturdy work boots from decades ago often lasted for years, and could be resoled multiple times. That’s because: - The sole was often a thick piece of vegetable-tanned leather, or a dense, hard rubber lug, attached by stitching (Goodyear welt) or nails. It was completely inflexible and dense, more like a tire tread in durability mindset. - There was no foam midsole to degrade. - The uppers were full-grain leather that could be conditioned and repaired. But here’s the key: those shoes were **not comfortable in the modern sense.** They had no shock absorption. Walking all day on pavement meant your feet, knees, and back took a pounding. People just accepted it as normal foot fatigue or “breaking in.” The reason they “lasted” was that they were essentially a hard, flat platform—very durable, but offering zero impact protection. ### 2. Foam isn’t a planned-obsolescence gimmick; it’s a medical leap Modern athletic and casual shoes started using EVA foam midsoles in the 1970s specifically because podiatrists and athletes were seeing alarming rates of stress fractures, shin splints, and joint problems from the rock-hard shoes of the past. The foam cushioning was a direct response to human need, not a plot to make shoes wear out faster. The foam does have a finite life—typically 300–500 miles—because it’s literally designed to sacrifice itself to protect your body. Each time it compresses, tiny cell walls break, gradually losing rebound. That’s why it “dies” long before the outsole rubber. But would you prefer a shoe that gives you joint pain after a few months but structurally “lasts” 5 years? Most people wouldn’t, which is why the market shifted. ### 3. If the old design was so great, why don’t we still use it? We do, actually. Goodyear-welted leather boots and handmade dress shoes still exist. But they’re niche, expensive, and most people hate wearing them for long because they’re heavy and unforgiving. They also typically don’t use soft foams, so they don’t provide modern cushioning. And crucially, *they still wear down*. Leather soles can get holes in them surprisingly quickly on pavement, and they’re slippery when wet. So they require regular resoling—a costly, time-consuming process that most modern consumers won’t tolerate. Longevity came via repair and upkeep, not invincibility. ### 4. Could we combine old-school durability with modern cushioning? We’re getting closer. Some hiking boots use dense polyurethane midsoles that last longer than EVA but are heavier. Some shoe foams are getting more resilient (like Adidas Boost TPU, which resists compression better). But the fundamental physical limit remains: any material soft enough to cushion your foot will degrade under repeated compression and shear. That’s not a corporate trick; it’s polymer physics. So your grandpa’s shoes did last longer in the sense that the structure held up. But they didn’t “last like tires”—they still wore out at the sole surface and needed resoling, and they certainly didn’t provide the protection modern shoes do. The switch to foam wasn’t a gimmick that took away durability; it was a conscious trade-off where comfort and health won out over a shoe that remains intact while slowly damaging your body. You can’t have both extreme durability *and* extreme cushioning with current materials. That’s the real story, not a lost golden age of shoe-making.
